  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 201 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA fairly active upper level pattern is expected across the United \nStates Wednesday onward, however, most of the systems are expected \nto track either north or south of the region. This will generally \nbring a cooler, drier weather pattern that settles in for the rest \nof the week. Wednesday and into the weekend, highs are only expected \nto climb into the 50s across most of western and north central \nNebraska, though a few highs clipping into the lower 60s cannot be \ncompletely ruled out. These highs are fairly typical for early \nNovember, as our climatological highs range between 55 to 58 \ndegrees. Lows each night are expected to drop into the lower 30s, \nwith some potential for localized drops into the upper 20s. Typical \nlows for early November are in the mid 20s, so this pattern may keep \nus just slightly warmer than normal. \n\nWill continue to monitor the potential for precipitation during the \nweekend, as an upper level disturbance is expected to track across \nthe Dakotas. Precipitation chances have remained fairly low \nconfidence, as there is still some uncertainty in how this pattern \nwill evolve. Latest guidance is trending the shortwave slightly \nfurther north, which may now only bring a glancing blow of \nprecipitation across the northern portions of the state. In fact, \nensemble guidance is now fairly pessimistic on northern Nebraska \nreceiving any precipitation. Latest probabilities from the GEFS for \nmeasureable rainfall are widely less than 20 percent for areas along \nand north of Highway 20, with some localized pockets of 30 percent \nchances along Highway 20. However, the European ensembles cover most \nof the region in a 30 percent chance for measureable rainfall this \nweekend. Even if precipitation were to occur, at this time, it looks \nto be very little. However, given the uncertainty, will continue to \nmonitor forecast trends and make changes to the forecast as \nappropriate.",
